1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,035
Keep both definitions where you can quote them, because the rest of this section

2
00:00:05,035 --> 00:00:09,779
leans on the difference, and because the confusion between them is how "we have a

3
00:00:09,779 --> 00:00:14,289
human in the loop" ends up meaning four different things in the same meeting.

4
00:00:14,289 --> 00:00:19,032
All four agents in this course stop at a draft, a flag, or a report waiting to be

5
00:00:19,032 --> 00:00:19,325
read.

6
00:00:19,325 --> 00:00:24,186
That was the right choice for everything you built, and Section 6 made the case for

7
00:00:24,186 --> 00:00:24,889
it directly.

8
00:00:24,889 --> 00:00:29,808
Not every useful agent can stay that cautious forever, and this lecture is about the

9
00:00:29,808 --> 00:00:34,669
next rung up: one that can act, but never without someone actually saying so first.

10
00:00:35,219 --> 00:00:37,979
Why teach this now, straight after the trials?

11
00:00:37,979 --> 00:00:41,158
Because of what a passed trial does to your appetite.

12
00:00:41,158 --> 00:00:46,077
A month of clean drafts makes the read-and-click step start to feel like ceremony,

13
00:00:46,077 --> 00:00:50,936
and the thought arrives on schedule: it has been right every time, why am I still

14
00:00:50,936 --> 00:00:51,836
the bottleneck?

15
00:00:51,836 --> 00:00:56,334
That thought is not wrong, it is premature until it has a design behind it.

16
00:00:56,334 --> 00:01:00,234
The honest path upward from draft-only is not removing the human.

17
00:01:00,234 --> 00:01:04,853
It is shrinking your involvement to one deliberate yes at the one moment that

18
00:01:04,853 --> 00:01:09,651
matters, and building that yes so it cannot happen by reflex, so that the ninety

19
00:01:09,651 --> 00:01:14,450
seconds you used to spend per output becomes five, without the one decision that

20
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:16,430
mattered ever leaving your hands.

21
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:20,989
That is an approval gate, and done properly it keeps most of the safety at a

22
00:01:20,989 --> 00:01:22,548
fraction of the attention.

23
00:01:22,548 --> 00:01:24,888
The difference sounds small and is not.

24
00:01:24,888 --> 00:01:29,507
Draft-only puts the entire decision in your hands, every time, no exceptions.

25
00:01:29,507 --> 00:01:34,246
An approval gate still trusts a "yes" once and then the action runs unattended,

26
00:01:34,246 --> 00:01:39,165
which means the gate itself has to be genuinely hard to click through by accident.

27
00:01:39,715 --> 00:01:44,503
Mechanically, a gate is one more node: the workflow pauses, sends you a message

28
00:01:44,503 --> 00:01:49,595
describing what it wants to do, and waits. n8n has wait-for-approval steps built for

29
00:01:49,595 --> 00:01:54,686
exactly this, and the details of wiring one are in its current docs rather than this

30
00:01:54,686 --> 00:01:56,989
video, for the usual freshness reason.

31
00:01:56,989 --> 00:02:01,899
What matters far more than the wiring is what lands in front of the person saying

32
00:02:01,899 --> 00:02:02,141
yes.

33
00:02:02,141 --> 00:02:07,233
You are not approving the agent's intention, you are approving the specific thing it

34
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:12,142
built, and that only means anything if you have actually seen it in the moment of

35
00:02:12,142 --> 00:02:12,748
approving.

36
00:02:12,748 --> 00:02:17,779
A yes given to a summary is a yes given on trust, and the gate exists precisely for

37
00:02:17,779 --> 00:02:20,507
the actions where trust alone was not enough.

38
00:02:20,507 --> 00:02:25,538
And the yes should live somewhere deliberate, a click in n8n itself or a reply in a

39
00:02:25,538 --> 00:02:30,387
channel you treat as work, never buried where notifications go to die, because a

40
00:02:30,387 --> 00:02:35,418
gate people approve without reading has already failed, it just has not been caught

41
00:02:35,418 --> 00:02:35,660
yet.

42
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:40,922
None of the four agents you built needed this yet, and that was deliberate.

43
00:02:40,922 --> 00:02:46,137
The moment you add one that does, this is the standard to hold it to: a gate a busy

44
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:49,152
afternoon cannot click through without noticing.

45
00:02:49,152 --> 00:02:54,367
Read down the middle column and notice what the test never asks: how good the agent

46
00:02:54,367 --> 00:02:54,555
is.

47
00:02:54,555 --> 00:02:55,875
That is its strength.

48
00:02:55,875 --> 00:03:01,152
Gate placement decided by trust evaporates as trust grows, which is exactly when the

49
00:03:01,152 --> 00:03:02,283
stakes are rising.

50
00:03:02,283 --> 00:03:07,372
Gate placement decided by reversibility stands still no matter how good the agent

51
00:03:07,372 --> 00:03:12,398
gets, because sent is still sent, and no week of flawless behaviour changes what

52
00:03:12,398 --> 00:03:13,089
sent means.

53
00:03:13,639 --> 00:03:18,918
Keep your answer to step three in writing, with the job description it belongs to,

54
00:03:18,918 --> 00:03:24,261
because a gate named in advance is a design and a gate improvised later is a patch,

55
00:03:24,261 --> 00:03:29,281
and Section 6.1's house-framing argument applies to gates exactly as it did to

56
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:29,668
locks.

57
00:03:29,668 --> 00:03:34,496
If you never build the more capable agent, the page cost you three minutes.

58
00:03:34,496 --> 00:03:39,710
If you do, it is the difference between adding autonomy to a plan and improvising

59
00:03:39,710 --> 00:03:41,512
autonomy onto a live system.

60
00:03:41,512 --> 00:03:46,726
Next lecture applies the same caution to something that feels smaller: adding one

61
00:03:46,726 --> 00:03:49,623
new capability to an agent you already trust.
